#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175701 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" :question/math-academy-id "e-5527" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/7dad070080562e459c926f5c8fa3d71a56981d68bd3ed4aae768c2f7450685a3.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/6786c7bc4d12bc3ee1a335eeb45967475850a585ec36805fe81aa76360d5a729.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/a73682dbe1dfccc14e5b3f0c68aa6e6aa7eb7a67bb475efbbaeaa1e886585554.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5528" :question/problem "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5529" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/e6a0d43572790d4daa49659c271c17032f2601458db0f6843257df07c245f372.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/9bf515d20e5a19646680474f0243dba793d1383eade4bf4986c7b11501baa800.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/1afe535b20d0fd1ce03b2b0886424a5ef1a29f4eea7e47c1aa818af67c98e868.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/af8f52f23a6fdcd1f070eff5876415cc884ffaafcbb2fe3f8737c31cc4761c86.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1525b61-1791-537e-a7c9-ed078a59c211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313259} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1e9fdf-66ef-5e8f-8c7d-5bec55e8637d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313260} {:answer/id #uuid "a6548834-4797-5767-916e-b73981f8869b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313261} {:answer/id #uuid "59255893-b8cf-5491-82ad-a934f5ad64b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313262} {:answer/id #uuid "edfb78f9-9410-5c9e-8ebf-b7ae1a265f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1525b61-1791-537e-a7c9-ed078a59c211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313259} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb31aa7-cb8b-575a-b421-246f08ecb24d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98eb69e4-fc5d-5211-9182-b032cfbab6b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140387" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5-5\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 10 & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-5-5\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}+(5-5\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+5\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1-5\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}{(-2)(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f52c4db2-db30-5cfa-b27a-85eebeed0b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313266} {:answer/id #uuid "88004dfe-67ba-56f0-9d8f-5512be6d7c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313267} {:answer/id #uuid "1470695f-ba3f-5e43-84cc-a8a23ddf7e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313268} {:answer/id #uuid "87a7d2bf-49de-565b-801b-be52dee20746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313269} {:answer/id #uuid "b03f300b-7654-5cc5-a73f-3412c060d3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f52c4db2-db30-5cfa-b27a-85eebeed0b54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313266} :answer-field/id #uuid "35b04317-56a9-5da5-afde-6d7e28ae797b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "314a48be-522b-5800-9e1c-46b3d1b94ba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38281" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\text{i} \\\\ -4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=2\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2389860e-eacc-53f0-99a9-ff8e7f1d6a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313273} {:answer/id #uuid "475d90ca-6526-5509-a066-c69bc7b5be6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313274} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1fc251-c546-581b-a335-489e08ef242f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\text{i}-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313275} {:answer/id #uuid "5307cab5-dcb2-52a8-8661-3bae6a8bea5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313276} {:answer/id #uuid "3324f838-65e2-5ea3-b3d1-16b9bf2c6a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5307cab5-dcb2-52a8-8661-3bae6a8bea5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313276} :answer-field/id #uuid "79bc9cf0-0fa1-5f71-8246-959f2e5296fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fddd2b24-ce03-5ec4-bc27-0a204f016dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-38287" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{-1}=\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f1dd336-9ead-5c21-ac6f-768a4eb24855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174901} {:answer/id #uuid "961a2918-8746-55b0-9f8a-3e0bf9ef7e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174902} {:answer/id #uuid "ba70b2fc-ceaa-5d8c-92ef-0cd4b5cd381f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174903} {:answer/id #uuid "c823c9ff-fca6-5959-89d1-86a2418f9e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ =-3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174904} {:answer/id #uuid "6cacf0bc-517f-5104-82fa-779e5d09c203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f1dd336-9ead-5c21-ac6f-768a4eb24855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174901} :answer-field/id #uuid "733f9f3a-1888-5241-8b1b-05a81df288bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6beb736-d02a-5b38-8238-42457ab554c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38475" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ 6-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ -6{\\text{i}}^{2}-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=3\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18520c3-2049-51c7-8fe5-765c1abe198a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174919} {:answer/id #uuid "db908152-aff0-5533-ab91-4b47d766321f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174920} {:answer/id #uuid "13d3d5e2-31cf-5838-803e-e3a849c8ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} {:answer/id #uuid "1d33a334-e204-5efd-b71c-1deeeaf61aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174922} {:answer/id #uuid "45f41a32-8463-5143-8a14-060c4971deb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d3d5e2-31cf-5838-803e-e3a849c8ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f3a2ce-5a9b-5da7-b976-ffe3fc139874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b212f7d8-65d4-5b14-984d-a9ab26791d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-38480" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2587ce7f-9706-5e5d-a6c3-342c2829bca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313280} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1af84f-e790-5068-9824-6806ba3c43b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313281} {:answer/id #uuid "303a7f35-fd09-52b5-966b-b4dc47f3c4fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313282} {:answer/id #uuid "ef08afdd-7f9a-5f5d-a465-b3dae78fb845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313283} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9bd114-1ed4-5b29-b1c6-5bc9d139a300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef08afdd-7f9a-5f5d-a465-b3dae78fb845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313283} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a33ffb-350c-5fb6-a6d3-ada3e904661d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2afbb4be-27ab-5a68-86cc-ed70968f1c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-38494" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b58f3722-0eec-5c35-8afd-3c298af03b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313287} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9a6031-d4e1-5654-8d4a-b8f273f5d414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313288} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff9013e-565a-51fe-aca9-2ced9a8a4db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313289} {:answer/id #uuid "630f0b2d-b380-58be-a4e3-573d90503d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313290} {:answer/id #uuid "4c84950a-915a-5c06-956d-67ee4d26150d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c84950a-915a-5c06-956d-67ee4d26150d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313291} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b6ffb3-a04c-54f7-937c-b612aff16d08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd776687-4060-5b96-97c0-411873a5bc30" :question/math-academy-id "q-39181" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-49-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}49{\\text{i}}^{2}-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=7\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b7e5abb-ba1b-5694-a51c-00b3f4716155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313294} {:answer/id #uuid "ed430d33-301c-5b50-9e48-bc43b4bb69a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313295} {:answer/id #uuid "293fa2ab-af7e-5899-bffd-fa294af061a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313296} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a088f0-e7a8-5b56-a1b8-b6f6b6d67000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313297} {:answer/id #uuid "578dbc7e-f6b3-5363-8f36-12dae84e5935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed430d33-301c-5b50-9e48-bc43b4bb69a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313295} :answer-field/id #uuid "4de5a998-ba1c-51f3-b9a1-49e7e489c1d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88f59971-0e5e-53c1-83ea-ae2fc365b9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81024" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\text{i}}^{2}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}(\\text{i}+1) \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(1+\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfab196-fbbe-5bbb-8882-73deacfad9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313301} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d65a70-d39e-5836-a745-ff424454a9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313302} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb3537c-94f8-56d2-a6c3-ea4f0dce3269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313303} {:answer/id #uuid "ba227c4e-9079-5da3-86c2-30143361bd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313304} {:answer/id #uuid "acc4784c-0bd0-5238-ac92-d37b8a309280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cb3537c-94f8-56d2-a6c3-ea4f0dce3269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313303} :answer-field/id #uuid "843ce327-8729-57ce-8689-9455632a4002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b512857-cfb0-5733-8d7f-8d3249677209" :question/math-academy-id "q-81025" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c30706ad-0a18-5f74-bf70-66516a72133b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313308} {:answer/id #uuid "ee913875-80f9-56ea-9efb-243406d0974c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313309} {:answer/id #uuid "4e79052b-86ba-5fae-a54b-16ceca49a124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313310} {:answer/id #uuid "2f999076-fcc6-5a81-89ac-02e07980bc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313311} {:answer/id #uuid "578aa672-7d71-518e-b9a9-3b0390797a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f999076-fcc6-5a81-89ac-02e07980bc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313311} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebe2346-ef3b-5f4b-9a56-15ffba17d184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a18d9711-0cae-5c70-a38a-d0ae5fc59b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-81027" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440d8e7a-64e3-530d-b388-b616283767e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313315} {:answer/id #uuid "06294dd6-235e-5814-83d7-7a32d002f6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313316} {:answer/id #uuid "523df79a-9229-549e-b163-4ddb8de44152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313317} {:answer/id #uuid "01e5e832-33e5-5be5-b594-58a317ab6fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313318} {:answer/id #uuid "ab46d392-035a-50a2-8df5-5137e4f2acb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06294dd6-235e-5814-83d7-7a32d002f6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313316} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28a7752-f6c4-5722-a84f-d4b0da47fca9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a5ec6fe-789c-5b17-b058-b3b284c2cf3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81031" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004f98a5-338b-5285-a123-795d807ce7ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313322} {:answer/id #uuid "77b6e6f4-8011-5c4b-a32c-8e48b4f17eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313323} {:answer/id #uuid "3e034af1-7a3e-56c2-8aca-cd2c4832bf9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313324} {:answer/id #uuid "4edab877-c126-5aba-a005-89744edada06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313325} {:answer/id #uuid "44e717ca-11c9-51f7-bfa7-273651a533d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e034af1-7a3e-56c2-8aca-cd2c4832bf9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313324} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df39464-1276-54bb-acf8-90f03b292028" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60d3c079-e063-599e-bac2-8b40e845bc8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81037" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-(-5)(5) & =0 \\\\ -15-5\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(10)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{-36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 6\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+3\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-3\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674e986c-e2ac-5641-925c-ecd1c1744134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313329} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4eb670-750c-5aab-8855-e03876e44163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313330} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c1ec67-82b8-5ced-b0f0-d9c3e847476f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313331} {:answer/id #uuid "552a0e35-2188-54f7-b088-11891c8ffc92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313332} {:answer/id #uuid "d558e086-d115-55d7-b77a-2d7575e7fecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b4eb670-750c-5aab-8855-e03876e44163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313330} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ed295fb-c5eb-502f-8c24-88a8c0bcebed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4cd2e17-94d5-5ebb-890a-ec926ba6d24e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81185" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{i}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b431d46-4be7-5db9-8c73-03f3c9fe89fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313336} {:answer/id #uuid "e84a0c7e-6bc8-50dd-b60b-80e86d74aef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313337} {:answer/id #uuid "94a82007-a00a-5d0a-bab3-33bb92f9d37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313338} {:answer/id #uuid "10bac6b1-60fc-5850-8080-5ca503a1cc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313339} {:answer/id #uuid "e190f602-b6ad-5288-8022-71c00a77c9c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b431d46-4be7-5db9-8c73-03f3c9fe89fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313336} :answer-field/id #uuid "0175c13b-88c7-585a-b8db-46e9f9877777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e94b55c-d751-5642-a64a-58a596312cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81187" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1+3\\text{i}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9921d574-2dc7-5723-ba9f-285a546e593a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313343} {:answer/id #uuid "8245439d-c6b3-5fcb-80b4-af49ec7f2e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313344} {:answer/id #uuid "d906558f-25fc-5767-82b1-eae98d88bae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313345} {:answer/id #uuid "c210fa92-6b03-5ddb-ab89-cc20a20c2433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313346} {:answer/id #uuid "d496f189-e88a-5dde-baa2-87abc9bb9a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c210fa92-6b03-5ddb-ab89-cc20a20c2433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313346} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f4e12a-3b1a-57d1-ab00-fc0000bea3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c243228-aa42-5f6c-be43-901ba6d02d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-81195" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+4\\text{i})(-2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
